<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Palatinate-Mosbach</h1><p class="st-meta">1410 CE – 1499 CE · capital Mosbach</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A minor Wittelsbach duchy on the Neckar and in the Odenwald, created when King Rupert's lands were divided in 1410.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupert-of-germany">King Rupert</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elector-palatine">Elector Palatine</a>, died in 1410, his lands were divided among his four sons. Otto I received the territory around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosbach">Mosbach</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neckar">Neckar</a> and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odenwald">Odenwald</a>, founding the Palatinate-Mosbach line, and in 1448 he also inherited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neumarkt-in-der-oberpfalz">Neumarkt</a> in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/upper-palatinate">Upper Palatinate</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosbach">Mosbach</a> dukes ruled a modest territory of small towns, forests and farms from Mosbach and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neumarkt-in-der-oberpfalz">Neumarkt</a>, closely tied to the senior electoral line at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a>. Their lands were scattered and their revenues small. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-ii-count-palatine-of-mosbach">Otto II</a>, the second and last duke, never married and left no heirs.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-ii-count-palatine-of-mosbach">Otto II</a> died in 1499, Palatinate-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosbach">Mosbach</a> passed back to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate</a>. Mosbach remained Palatine until 1803, when it went to the princes of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-leiningen">Leiningen</a> and then to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/grand-duchy-of-baden">Grand Duchy of Baden</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/palatinate-mosbach.jpg" alt="Palatinate-Mosbach" width="320" height="205"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mosbach-1645-Merian.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1410 CE – 1499 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mosbach</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Otto I, Otto II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Pfalz-Mosbach</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1454&amp;focus=palatinate-mosbach" aria-label="Palatinate-Mosbach on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4890 457 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Palatinate-Mosbach lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5210 713L5200 712"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5200 712h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5210 713h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5210 713h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Mosbach. <a href="/atlas?year=1454&amp;focus=palatinate-mosbach">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
